# Contributing to djotls

Thank you for your interest in contributing to djotls!

## Build

``` bash
cargo build
```

## Test

``` bash
cargo test
```

## Releases

The `package.version` in `Cargo.toml` drives stable and prerelease
versions.
Update it and `Cargo.lock` together, then merge to `master`.
After the same commit passes the `pass` job, CI calls
`.github/workflows/cd.yml`.
The shared GoReleaser action validates the version, creates and pushes
`v<version>`, publishes GitHub Release archives, and publishes the crate
to crates.io using Trusted Publishing.

Each archive includes `djotfmt` and `djotls`.
The configured targets are Linux and macOS, on x86_64 and aarch64.
The project installs `cargo-zigbuild` in the `before.hooks` of
`.goreleaser.yaml`.

For an RC, use a version such as `0.1.1-rc.1`.
Tags are created automatically for both stable and RC versions;
`release.prerelease: auto` marks GitHub prereleases.
Release jobs run one at a time.
If publication fails after tagging, use {*Re-run failed jobs*} on the
original CI run to retry the same commit.
Versions already published to crates.io are skipped.
PR checks, scheduled checks and manual CI runs do not publish.

### Trusted Publisher setup

Before merging the release workflow, configure a Trusted Publisher
for the `djotls` crate on crates.io:

- Repository owner: `black-desk`

- Repository name: `djotls`

- Workflow filename: `ci.yml`

- Environment: leave unset

The initial crate version has already been published manually.
The workflow filename is `ci.yml` because it is the caller whose
`workflow_ref` appears in the GitHub OIDC token, even though the
publishing job is defined in the reusable `cd.yml`.
Both the caller and publishing job grant `id-token: write`;
no long-lived crates.io token is needed.

## Debug

djotfmt uses the `log` crate for logging.
You can increase the verbosity level with the `-v` flag
to see more detailed information about the formatting process.

- {_(default)_} Warn: Only warnings and errors.

- `-v` Info: General informational messages.

- `-vv` Debug: Show jotdown parse events and the corresponding source
  text.

- `-vvv` Trace: Show internal rendering state: pending words, pending
  lines, prefix stack, etc.

For example, to debug the formatting of a file with full trace output:

``` bash
cargo run -- -vvv input.dj
```

At `Debug` level you will see output like:

```
DEBUG Event: Start(Paragraph, ...)
DEBUG Source: "hello world"
DEBUG Attributes: ...
```

At `Trace` level you will see additional internal state:

```
TRACE Pending word: "hello"
TRACE Commit word: "hello"
TRACE Pending line: "hello "
TRACE Prefix: ["> "]
```
